@lang en
IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `ca-interpretation-time`

-- ca-tests-periods.l4
--
-- Periods counted from a day (sections 27, 28 and 37(1)) and the arithmetic around them.
-- Every expected value below was written from the text BEFORE the module was run (NOTES.md, section 4 records the first seven);
-- none was copied from the module's output.  The weekday notes were checked with a calendar outside L4 (python3 `datetime`).
--
-- Each row of the table is asserted five ways: the period runs from its first day to its last day, the specified day is outside it,
-- the first day and the last day are inside it, and the day after the last day is outside it.

GIVEN p IS A `A period`
      a IS A DATE
      b IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
p `runs from` a `to` b MEANS
        (p's `the first day`) EQUALS a
    AND (p's `the last day`) EQUALS b

§ `three years after 2025-07-16 (worked case) [ss 37(1), 28]`

#ASSERT (`the period of` 3 `years after` (YMD 2025 7 16)) `runs from` (YMD 2025 7 17) `to` (YMD 2028 7 16)
#ASSERT NOT ((YMD 2025 7 16) `falls inside` (`the period of` 3 `years after` (YMD 2025 7 16)))
#ASSERT (YMD 2025 7 17) `falls inside` (`the period of` 3 `years after` (YMD 2025 7 16))
#ASSERT (YMD 2028 7 16) `falls inside` (`the period of` 3 `years after` (YMD 2025 7 16))
#ASSERT NOT ((YMD 2028 7 17) `falls inside` (`the period of` 3 `years after` (YMD 2025 7 16)))

